= Mathematical Equations

== Inline and Display Equations

Typst has a built-in maths mode that is simpler to write than LaTeX. Everything between dollar signs `$ ... $` is maths.

#example(```typ
The area of a circle is $A = pi r^2$, written inside the sentence.

To put an equation on its own line, centred, leave a space
just inside the dollar signs:

$ A = pi r^2 $
```)

`$x$` without spaces stays *inline*, inside the sentence. `$ x $` with spaces becomes a *display* equation on its own line.

== The Basics

#example(```typ
Powers and indices: $x^2$, $x_i$, $x_(i+1)^(2n)$ \
Fractions: $a/b$, $(a + b)/(c + d)$, $1/2$ \
Roots: $sqrt(x)$, $root(3, x)$ \
Operators: $a dot b$, $a times b$, $a plus.minus b$, $a div b$ \
Comparisons: $a = b$, $a != b$, $a <= b$, $a >= b$, $a approx b$ \
Functions: $sin x$, $cos theta$, $log n$, $exp(x)$, $max(a, b)$
```)

When a superscript, subscript or fraction covers more than one character, wrap it in round brackets, like `x_(i+1)`. The brackets themselves are not printed.

== Greek Letters and Symbols

Write a symbol's name to print it. No backslash is needed, unlike in LaTeX.

