== Sums, Products, Integrals and Limits

#example(```typ
$ sum_(i=1)^n x_i quad
  product_(i=1)^n x_i quad
  integral_0^infinity e^(-x) dif x quad
  lim_(x -> 0) (sin x) / x = 1 $
```)

`quad` adds a wide space between the parts, and `dif` writes the upright "d" used in integrals.

== Brackets, Matrices and Vectors

Brackets grow automatically to fit what is inside them.

#example(```typ
$ (1 + 1/n)^n quad [a, b] quad {x : x > 0} quad abs(x) quad norm(bold(v)) $

$ A = mat(1, 2, 3; 4, 5, 6; 7, 8, 9) quad
  B = mat(delim: "[", a, b; c, d) quad
  bold(x) = vec(x_1, x_2, x_3) $
```)

Inside `mat(...)`, commas separate the columns and semicolons separate the rows.

== Text, Accents and Cases

#example(```typ
$ "accuracy" = "correct predictions" / "total predictions" $

$ hat(y) quad overline(x) quad tilde(z) quad dot(x) quad arrow(v) quad cal(L) quad bb(R) $

$ "ReLU"(x) = cases(
  x & "if " x > 0,
  0 & "otherwise",
) $
```)

Text in quotation marks is printed as normal words. Without the quotes, each letter would be treated as a separate variable.

== Multi-line Equations

Use `\` to break a line and `&` to mark where the lines should line up (usually just before the `=` sign):

#example(```typ
$ (a + b)^2 &= (a + b)(a + b) \
            &= a^2 + a b + b a + b^2 \
            &= a^2 + 2 a b + b^2 $
```)

== Numbering and Referring to Equations

Every display equation is numbered automatically by chapter, like (3.1), as the college rules require: the first equation of Chapter 3 is (3.1), the next (3.2), and so on. In appendices the numbers become (A1.1), (A1.2), …

To refer to an equation, give it a label and write `@eqt:` followed by the label name. The reference is printed as "Eqn.(3.1)":

#example(```typ
$ sigma(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z)) $ <sigmoid>

The sigmoid function in @eqt:sigmoid maps any number to the range 0 to 1.
```)

#tip[To leave one display equation unnumbered (for example an intermediate step), give it the label `<->`, like this: `$ x = 2 $ <->`]

== Common Mistakes

#table(
  columns: (2.6cm, 2.6cm, 1fr),
  stroke: 0.5pt,
  align: (center + horizon, center + horizon, left + horizon),
  table.header([*Wrong*], [*Right*], [*Why*]),
  [`$xy$`], [`$x y$`], [Letters next to each other are read as one name, which causes the error "unknown variable". Separate them with a space.],
  [`$a*b$`], [`$a dot b$`], [`*` prints an asterisk ($a*b$). Use `dot` ($a dot b$) or `times` ($a times b$).],
  [`$e^-x$`], [`$e^(-x)$`], [A superscript only takes the next item. Wrap longer powers in round brackets.],
  [`$1/2x$`], [`$1/(2x)$`], [Without brackets, only the item right next to `/` is divided.],
  [`Costs $5`], [`Costs \$5`], [A `$` in normal text starts maths mode. Write `\$` to print a dollar sign.],
)

== Examples from AI and Data Science

#example(```typ
Softmax:
$ "softmax"(z_i) = e^(z_i) / (sum_(j=1)^K e^(z_j)) $

Binary cross-entropy loss:
$ cal(L) = -1/N sum_(i=1)^N [y_i log hat(y)_i + (1 - y_i) log(1 - hat(y)_i)] $

Gradient descent update:
$ theta_(t+1) = theta_t - eta nabla_theta cal(L)(theta_t) $

Bayes' theorem:
$ P(A | B) = (P(B | A) thin P(A)) / P(B) $

F1 score:
$ F_1 = 2 dot ("Precision" dot "Recall") / ("Precision" + "Recall") $

Scaled dot-product attention:
$ "Attention"(Q, K, V) = "softmax"((Q K^T) / sqrt(d_k)) V $
```)

